      "page_title": "REST and HTTP Fundamentals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q1. What is a REST API and what are its core constraints?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==REST== is an architectural style: clients act on **resources**, named by URLs, through stateless HTTP calls.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Key points",
          "items": [
            "Client–server: UI and storage evolve apart",
            "__Stateless__: each request carries its own context",
            "Cacheable: responses declare if reusable",
            "Uniform interface: URIs, verbs, representations, HATEOAS",
            "Layered system; code on demand is optional"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q2. What is the difference between REST and SOAP?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "REST is a style over plain HTTP, usually JSON. ==SOAP== is a strict XML protocol with an envelope and a WSDL contract.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Key points",
          "items": [
            "SOAP is XML only; REST allows JSON, XML, more",
            "SOAP has built-in WS-Security and transactions",
            "REST reuses HTTP caching; SOAP POSTs rarely cache",
            "SOAP for strict enterprise contracts, REST for public APIs"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q3. What does it mean that HTTP is stateless, and how do you manage state anyway?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The server keeps **no** client context between requests, so every request must be self-contained.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Key points",
          "items": [
            "Client sends state: `Authorization` header, JWT, cookie",
            "Or store a session in Redis, keyed by session ID",
            "Resource state lives in the database, not in memory",
            "__Stateless__ servers scale behind any load balancer"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q4. What is the difference between PUT and PATCH?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==PUT== replaces the whole resource and is idempotent. ==PATCH== applies a partial change to it.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Key points",
          "items": [
            "PUT: fields you omit get reset or nulled",
            "PATCH sends only changed fields (Merge Patch, JSON Patch)",
            "PATCH is idempotent only if designed so: **increment** is not"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "http",
          "lines": [
            "PUT /users/7",
            "{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"age\":30}",
            "",
            "PATCH /users/7",
            "{\"age\":31}"
          ],
          "note": "PUT omitting a field wipes it"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q5. What is the difference between REST and GraphQL?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "REST exposes many endpoints with a fixed shape. ==GraphQL== has one endpoint where the client picks the fields.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Key points",
          "items": [
            "GraphQL avoids over- and under-fetching in one query",
            "REST caches easily over HTTP; GraphQL needs persisted queries",
            "GraphQL often returns `200` with errors in the body",
            "REST is simpler for CRUD; GraphQL suits varied clients"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "apigateway",
      "subtitle": "Q1 to Q5"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Safe Methods and Core Status Codes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q6. What is the difference between safe and idempotent HTTP methods?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==Safe== methods never change server state. ==Idempotent== methods can be repeated and leave the same end state as one call.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every safe method is idempotent, not the reverse",
            "`DELETE` twice: 2nd call may be 404, state same",
            "Idempotent means clients can **retry** after a timeout",
            "`POST` is neither, so it needs an Idempotency-Key"
          ],
          "style": "star"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Method",
            "Safe",
            "Idempotent"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "GET, HEAD",
              "yes",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "PUT",
              "no",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "DELETE",
              "no",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "POST",
              "no",
              "no"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q7. What is the difference between 401 Unauthorized and 403 Forbidden?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`401` means the server does not know who you are. `403` means it knows you, and the answer is **no**.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`401` carries a `WWW-Authenticate` header",
            "Logging in again fixes a 401, never a 403",
            "Some APIs return 404 to hide a resource exists"
          ],
          "style": "star"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Situation",
            "Code"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "No token sent",
              "401"
            ],
            [
              "Expired JWT",
              "401"
            ],
            [
              "Role lacks admin",
              "403"
            ],
            [
              "Another user's order",
              "403 / 404"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q8. When do you return 200, 201, 202 and 204?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`200` is success with a body, `201` is a new resource created, `202` is accepted but not finished, `204` is success with no body.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`201` should add a `Location` header for the new URL",
            "`202` returns a status URL to poll for the job",
            "`204` suits `DELETE` and `PUT` that echo nothing"
          ],
          "style": "star"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Typical use",
            "Body"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "200",
              "GET result",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "201",
              "POST created",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "202",
              "Queued job",
              "optional"
            ],
            [
              "204",
              "DELETE done",
              "none"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Q9. What is the difference between 400, 404, 409 and 422?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`400` is a malformed request, `404` is a missing resource, `409` clashes with current state, `422` is well-formed but fails validation.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`409`: duplicate email or stale version on update",
            "`400` vs `422` is a convention, so stay **consistent**",
            "Return a JSON error body naming the bad field"
          ],
          "style": "star"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "400",
              "Broken JSON body"
            ],
            [
              "404",
              "`GET /users/999` absent"
            ],
            [
              "409",
              "Email already registered"
            ],
            [
              "422",
              "`age: -5` fails rules"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Clients may turn a POST into GET on 301 or 302",
            "Use `308` when an API endpoint moves for good",
            "Browsers and CDNs cache permanent redirects hard"
          ],
          "style": "star"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "apigateway",
